{"id": "azulejo-cobalt-wash-bleed", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 2, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["The finished panel reads as a stock azulejo tile graphic: diamond medallions, a dotted frame and a centre dot, so it draws the tile itself.", "The idea promises loose cobalt washes that bleed, but the render shows hard-edged vector diamonds with flat fills and no pigment pooling or granulation, so it does not read as handmade.", "The panel is cropped hard into the corner, so the figure that should appear at four-tile scale is never seen whole and looks like a clipped sticker.", "The first third of the cycle is a blank cream field, which is a long reveal with nothing to look at."], "fix": "Keep the corner placement and glaze white field, but abstract the azulejo grammar: hairline square seams with a figure that only resolves across a 2x2 block, and a thin border band a quarter of a tile wide, with no literal quatrefoil or medallion. Render the cobalt as real wet-on-wet wash, using noise-driven edge diffusion, darker pigment pooling at edges and slight granulation that crosses the seams. Show a faint seam grid from frame one so the reveal never starts blank, and aim for illustrative-handmade: one hand, one cobalt and one ochre point."}
```

