{"id": "deck-deal-slide", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 2, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["It draws a stack of rounded cards, a picture of the deck, so a shader card ends up sitting behind or beside the real plan card it is meant to support.", "The soft-3d lighting is weak: a flat blue-grey slab with a faint top sheen. There is no believable light or gloss and no label, so it reads as a placeholder.", "Across the frames the step out and sink back barely registers. The 10 second cycle shows almost no visible dealt advance.", "The slate blue slab on near-black, with the green support colour unused, feels generic rather than taken from the felt-table source."], "fix": "Keep the dealing grammar but stop drawing the card. Render only a quiet tone-step offset: one or two hairline bands that slide out one step with a quick custom ease and settle, peeking out from under where the real UI card will sit. Use a deep felt-green matte ground with fine grain and the gold only as one thin edge glint. If it stays soft-3d-objects, make it one well lit tactile slab with real light falloff and room for a real label, not a stack."}
