{"id": "caramel-thread-nest", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 2, "literal": false, "problems": ["Strands read as straight vector rays fanning from a point, closer to a light-burst or whisk than pulled sugar; the droop and taper barely show.", "The promised hand-inked wobble and print feel are invisible, so it does not deliver illustrative-handmade: clean digital lines with no hand.", "The convergence point is clipped hard against the right edge, so the focal knot is lost and the composition feels cropped.", "The flat brown ground has no second-voice texture, and the slightly muddy mid-brown sits off the dark-first bar."], "fix": "Borrow the real behaviour of pulled sugar: strands that sag in gravity curves, thin to near-nothing at the tips, and cross loosely so they gather into a light, sparse mesh. Inset the anchor so the gathered knot is visible. Give the line a true ink weight (taper, slight jitter and pooled ends), push the ground toward near-black caramel with fine grain, and keep the single amber strand as the only accent, aiming for illustrative-handmade."}
